\description{
These functions coerce networks into the 'stocnet' class of \code{{manynet}},
and from it into the data objects of the modelling packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{as_stocnet()} coerces the object into a 'stocnet' object
(see \code{\link[=make_stocnet]{make_stocnet()}}).
\item \code{as_siena()} coerces the object into a \code{{RSiena}} 'sienadata' object.
\item \code{as_monan()} coerces a two-mode network of people and places
into a \code{{MoNAn}} 'processState.monan' object.
}

An effort is made for all of these coercion routines to be as lossless
as possible, though some object classes are better at retaining certain
kinds of information than others.
}
\section{Text}{

\code{as_stocnet()} reads a document-feature matrix of \code{{quanteda}} ('dfm')
and a term-document or document-term matrix of \code{{tm}}
('TermDocumentMatrix', 'DocumentTermMatrix').
Each gives a two-mode network of "documents" and "terms",
with the count (or other weighting) of each term in each document as the
weight of its tie, as \code{\link[=collect_text]{collect_text()}} gives.
The document variables of a 'dfm' become attributes of the documents.
Neither \code{{quanteda}} nor \code{{tm}} needs to be installed.
}

\section{Records}{

\code{as_stocnet()} reads the bibliographic records of \code{{bibliometrix}}
('bibliometrixDB', as \code{bibliometrix::convert2df()} gives)
as \code{\link[=collect_bibliography]{collect_bibliography()}} does,
into a multimodal network of works, authors, keywords, and sources.
The other coercions, such as \code{as_igraph()} and \code{as_tidygraph()},
read the records through \code{as_stocnet()},
so the functions of the stocnet packages take the records directly.
The records cannot be rebuilt from a network,
so a function that would return the class it was given
returns a 'stocnet' for them.
\code{{bibliometrix}} does not need to be installed.
}

\section{Mobility}{

\code{as_monan()} takes a two-mode network,
with people as the first mode and places as the second mode.
The ties between people and places say where each person is.
They have \code{begin} and \code{end} times, or the \code{time} of each wave of a panel.
A person holds one place at a time.
\code{as_monan()} takes the place of each person at \code{time1} and \code{time2},
and gives these elements:
\itemize{
\item \code{moves}, the dependent variable, with one row for each person:
the place at \code{time1} and the place at \code{time2}.
A person who stays has the same place twice.
\item \code{people} and \code{places}, the two node sets.
\item each numeric attribute of the people or of the places,
under its own name.
\item each layer of ties among the places, such as adjacency or distance,
under its layer name.
}

\code{as_monan()} also takes a network of places with an \code{occupant} record,
as \code{\link[=play_mobility]{play_mobility()}} gives.
The occupants are then the people,
and the attributes of the occupants at the start are
the attributes of the people.
The start is time 0.

A person who has no place at one of the two times is not included.
MoNAn requires that every place occurs in \code{moves},
so a place that no person holds at either time is removed.
See also \code{\link[=to_mobility]{to_mobility()}}, which gives the moves as a network of places.
}

